  "solution": "<p>The right-hand side is nonnegative, so solutions are restricted to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. The parabola <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> has roots <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and 3 and vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p>The graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has period 2 and range <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> its heights are 4,0,4,0,4. Sketching both standard curves together shows a crossing between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and 0, a touching point at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a crossing between 2 and 3. The two outer crossings are symmetric about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Thus there are three distinct solutions. This is the graph-sketch approach used by the official worked answer; no trigonometric differentiation is needed.</p><p><strong>Answer B: 3.</strong></p>",
